{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' test/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_harness.cljc && cat collab/exception-track/1794396000000-mob-record-mayfail-depth.md && git rev-parse master origin/master\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' test/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_harness.cljc && cat collab/exception-track/1794396000000-mob-record-mayfail-depth.md && git rev-parse master origin/master\"","aggregated_output":"(ns yang.typescript.antlr.linked-harness\n  \"The linked profile's composition for the JavaScript tests (T1 step S5,\n   docs/design/yang.typescript.md section 13): the namespace that requires\n   this is the publisher of `js` and `jssp`, so it publishes each module\n   once per process into one in-memory content store and serves them to\n   every linked run over a `yin.vm.linker.interpreter` composition under\n   `:trusted` derivation (the remediation ruling's R6). The `:verifying`\n   evidence is `publish-module!`'s own `link-local` links, which\n   `linked-prelude-test` asserts.\n\n   A linked run builds the program's task on one of the three vector VMs\n   with a link pair, then runs the VM and serves the pair alternately\n   until the VM stops blocking, as the REPL's drive does. The AST walker\n   is not a backend here: its link of `js` is refused `:undeclared-free`\n   until L-b.\"\n  (:require [yin.vm.semantic.linearize :as sr-linearize]\n            [yin.vm.semantic.derive :as derive]\n            [dao.jing.mem :as mem]\n            [yang.core.call :as call]\n            [yang.typescript.antlr.prelude :as prelude]\n            [yang.typescript.antlr.safepoint :as safepoint]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.data :as data]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-code :as dcode]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-register-code :as rcode]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.register :as rvm]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as dvm]\n            [yin.vm.integer :as integer]\n            [yin.vm.linker.interpreter :as link]\n            [yin.vm.linker.publish :as publish]\n            [yin.vm.module :as module]\n            [yin.vm.semantic :as semantic]\n            [yin.vm.test-utils :as tu]))\n\n\n(def integer-limits\n  \"The ordinary JavaScript composition's `integer` limits.\"\n  {::integer/max-bits 100000, ::integer/max-digits 4300})\n\n\n(defn registry\n  \"The full host registry a linked JavaScript composition registers: the\n   `require` handler, then `cell`, `data`, `integer` under `limits`, and\n   `stream` — the hook module `jssp` polls a signal stream, so the\n   safepointed composition needs it even though `js` does not.\"\n  ([] (registry integer-limits))\n  ([limits]\n   (-> (module/default-registry)\n       module/register-cell-module\n       (data/register-data-module {::data/max-items 1048576})\n       (prelude/register-integer-module limits)\n       module/register-stream-module)))\n\n\n(def published\n  \"`js` published once into its own content store:\n   `{:store s :result r :address a :call-address ca}`. `yang.call` is\n   published first so `js` pins its shared calling runtime.\"\n  (delay\n    (let [store (mem/create-content-mem)\n          call-result (publish/publish-module! store (call/module-spec (registry)))\n          result (publish/publish-module! store\n                                          (prelude/module-spec (registry)\n                                                               (:address call-result)))]\n      {:store store,\n       :result result,\n       :address (:address result),\n       :call-address (:address call-result)})))\n\n\n(def published-jssp\n  \"`jssp` published once into `js`'s own content store, which its pinned\n   requirement already lives in: the same shape as `published`.\"\n  (delay\n    (let [{:keys [store address]} @published\n          result (publish/publish-module!\n                   store (safepoint/module-spec (registry) address))]\n      {:store store, :result result, :address (:address result)})))\n\n\n(defn source\n  \"The serving composition over the published store: `name-env` defaults\n   to `{js <address> yang.call <address>}`, derivation `:trusted`, the test\n   being the publisher. `jssp`-serving sources pass their own `name-env`.\"\n  ([] (source {'js (:address @published),\n               'yang.call (:call-address @published)}))\n  ([name-env]\n   (link/composition {:content-store (:store @published),\n                      :name-env name-env,\n                      :derivation :trusted})))\n\n\n(defn safepoint-source\n  \"The serving composition over the published modules: `jssp` requires\n   `js`, and `js` requires `yang.call`.\"\n  []\n  (source {'js (:address @published),\n           'jssp (:address @published-jssp),\n           'yang.call (:call-address @published)}))\n\n\n(defn publish-guest-module!\n  \"One guest module's `spec` (lower/module-spec's) published into `js`'s\n   own content store, where its pinned requirements already live:\n   `publish-module!`'s answer. Guest modules are tiny beside `js`, so a\n   test may publish its own; publish imports bottom-up, the `deps` map\n   naming each import's address.\"\n  [spec]\n  (publish/publish-module! (:store @published) spec))\n\n\n(def backends\n  \"Each vector backend's root task over a program AST and composition.\"\n  {:semantic (fn [ast opts]\n               (semantic/load-ast (semantic/create-vm opts)\n                                  (vm/ast->datoms ast)\n                                  vm/ast-contract)),\n   :stack (fn [ast opts]\n            (dvm/create-vm (:image (derive/stack-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast))))\n                           (assoc opts :contract vm/stack-contract))),\n   :register (fn [ast opts]\n               (rvm/create-vm (derive/register-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast)))\n                              (assoc opts :contract vm/register-contract)))})\n\n\n(def continues\n  \"Each vector backend's admission of the next top-level unit to a halted\n   task, as `yin.repl` admits an input line: the task keeps its store,\n   heap, module registry and module stores.\"\n  {:semantic (fn [task ast]\n               (semantic/load-ast task (vm/ast->datoms ast) vm/ast-contract)),\n   :stack (fn [task ast]\n            (let [img (:image (derive/stack-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast))))\n                  attached (dvm/attach-image task img vm/stack-contract)]\n              (assoc attached\n                     :pc (dvm/absolute-pc attached [(dcode/image-hash img) 0])\n                     :frames [] :stack [] :continuation []\n                     :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil))),\n   :register (fn [task ast]\n               (let [img (derive/register-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast)))\n                     attached (rvm/attach-image task img vm/register-contract)\n                     pc (rvm/absolute-pc attached\n                                         [(rcode/register-hash img) 0])\n                     body (some #(when (= pc (:start %)) %)\n                                (:bodies (:segment attached)))]\n                 (assoc attached\n                        :pc pc :frames [] :continuation []\n                        :registers (vec (repeat (:registers body) nil))\n                        :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil)))})\n\n\n(defn composition\n  \"A task's composition over `modules` and the link `pair`.\"\n  [modules pair]\n  {:make-stream tu/make-stream,\n   :capability-secret tu/secret,\n   :secret-source (fn [origin] (str tu/secret \"/\" (name origin))),\n   :primitives vm/primitives,\n   :modules modules,\n   :link-request (:requests pair),\n   :link-response (:responses pair)})\n\n\n(defn drive\n  \"Run `task` and serve `pair` from `src` alternately until the task\n   stops blocking, at most 40 rounds. Returns `[task pair]`.\"\n  [task pair src]\n  (loop [task (vm/run task)\n         pair pair\n         i 0]\n    (if (and (vm/blocked? task) (< i 40))\n      (let [pair (:pair (link/serve {:pair pair, :source src}))]\n        (recur (vm/run task) pair (inc i)))\n      [task pair])))\n\n\n(defn run-linked\n  \"The finished task of the linked program `ast` on `backend`. Options:\n   `:modules` (default the full registry), `:link-source` (default the\n   `:trusted` composition over `js`), and `:prep`, applied to the task\n   before it runs (a composition handing the task its streams, binding\n   `jssp`'s signal stream among them). A refused link throws as the task's\n   own error.\"\n  ([backend ast] (run-linked backend ast {}))\n  ([backend ast {:keys [modules link-source prep]}]\n   (let [pair (link/make-pair)\n         task ((get backends backend)\n               ast\n               (composition (or modules (registry)) pair))]\n     (first (drive (if prep (prep task) task)\n                   pair (or link-source (source)))))))\n\n\n(defn runners-under\n  \"Each vector VM's runner of a linked program over `modules`: the\n   program's value.\"\n  [modules]\n  (into {}\n        (map (fn [k]\n               [k (fn [ast]\n                    (vm/value (run-linked k ast {:modules modules})))]))\n        (keys backends)))\n\n\n(def runners\n  \"The linked runners under the ordinary limits.\"\n  (runners-under (registry)))\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-11 12:00:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-11 19:00:00 +07 (+0700)\nStatus: MOB RECORD — 2/2 CORRECT WITH AMENDMENTS (astra + deepseek-flash, independent); steward\nsynthesis follows. Supersedes the steward's framing in this thread where amended.\n\n# MOD RECORD — the may-fail chain depth claim\n\nBrief: `1794395000000-architect-mod-mayfail-depth.prompt.md`. Verdicts:\n`…gpt-6-astra.stdout.log`, `…deepseek-flash.stdout.log`.\n\n## Where both architects agree (the corrected claim of record)\n\n1. **Bottom line holds**: the continuation encoding's non-tail try bodies make may-fail recursion\n   grow linearly; this is currently unmeasured for py/ts/php at the kit level; the yang.clojure\n   port inherits it; a measured uniform probe is warranted.\n2. **The outcome model is ALSO non-tail through nested `try`** — both architects found this\n   independently. Its constant depth covers only HANDLER-FREE tail chains (chains whose\n   intermediate calls don't open a try). So \"may-fail chains\" was over-broad in the steward's\n   framing: the distinguishing property is handler-free tail propagation, not may-fail per se.\n3. **\"Every published entry\" is currently false for PHP**: its function/Closure entries tail-apply\n   `:code` with no bridge — a known R1 implementation gap, not a safe elision. The claim becomes\n   true for php when its round lands the bridge. (Growth in php today comes from return escapes.)\n4. **Native-runtime parity is the wrong justification**: CPython 3.11+/V8/Zend use exception\n   tables (zero-cost — entering a try adds no frame; astra measured Python/Node with-try vs\n   without: no additional logical frames). Deepseek adds the design-level point: yin.vm\n   deliberately makes continuations heap data with no overflow, so the encoding is honest to the\n   DESIGN, not to the source runtimes.\n5. **The uniform probe**: assert ONLY the constant case (loop/recur and handler-free chains:\n   `D(16)=D(64)=D(256)`); measure and report the linear case (depth increments at 8/32/128 across\n   three categories: outer-catch calls, cross-unit published calls, nested handlers) with an\n   upper-bound ceiling — NEVER contractually require growth (\"a lower-bound assertion requiring\n   growth would reject a correct optimization\"). Also: python already has a loop-safepoint depth\n   pin (safepoint_test.cljc:420) — the \"no probe exists\" claim is narrowed to handler-chain probes.\n6. **Neither architect would override the owner's veto on architectural authority.** Both treat\n   the LaTTe depth measurement as the evidence gate before the port merges.\n\n## The amendments that change the port brief\n\n1. **The regression is BROADER and SHARPER than \"handler scopes\"**: deepseek — entry bridges AND\n   return escapes make **every value-returning recursion across published entries** linear; loops\n   are constant only because the loop self-call sits outside the try body. Astra: \"depth\n   proportional to simultaneously active scopes/bridges, not completed try executions.\"\n2. **The genuinely first-order workload class** (both): search/backtracking engines — long\n   deterministic tail-call stretches between choice points where failure is normal control flow.\n   `loop`/`recur` restructuring is NOT a local substitution for mutual/supplied functions.\n3. **The remedy without the VM seam** (deepseek): **preserve an intra-unit private tail lane in\n   the port** — intra-unit calls that don't cross a published boundary keep direct tail\n   application; only published/cross-unit entries take the bridge frame. No VM change; consistent\n   with the declined seam.\n4. **The owner-visibility threshold** (deepseek): any LaTTe corpus workload whose max continuation\n   depth grows >10× pre-port, or exceeds 10,000 frames post-port, must be shown to the owner\n   before the port merges. Astra's version: outcome baseline completes within an agreed resource\n   budget where the port does not, AND extensibility prevents a local rewrite — with the actual\n   call path, sizes, peak depth, memory, runtime recorded on both implementations.\n5. **The LaTTe harness lacks depth collection** (astra, verified at latte_oracle_test.clj:239) —\n   a depth-collecting harness is a PREREQUISITE of the port's acceptance, not a re-run.\n6. **Acceptance tree must be identified-clean** — the voided contaminated result cannot establish\n   anything (astra reiterated against the acceptance report's correction).\n\n## Steward synthesis\n\nThe port proceeds under the owner's clarified veto, with these amendments as binding acceptance\ncriteria: (i) LaTTe depth harness built FIRST, baselines recorded pre-port; (ii) intra-unit\nprivate tail lane preserved in the port design (no VM change); (iii) uniform probe asserts the\nconstant case, measures the linear case; (iv) the deepseek thresholds escalate to the owner before\nmerge; (v) PHP's missing bridge noted — the \"every published entry\" property of the unified\nencoding becomes true when the php round lands, completing the uniformity the owner asked for.\n56cc5ca36855c0d047c11b7658e79cfc27d238e6\n56cc5ca36855c0d047c11b7658e79cfc27d238e6\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
